{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.379,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.12,
            0.886,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": "Guna and Anshu started wondering how this simple method is able to find prime numbers! Think how this method works. Read the steps given above again and see what happens after each step is carried out."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.2,
            0.353,
            0.222
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.23,
            0.885,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "1. We see that 2 is a prime and also an even number. Is there any other even prime?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.275,
            0.885,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "2. Look at the list of primes till 100. What is the smallest difference between two successive primes? What is the largest difference?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.321,
            0.885,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "3. Are there an equal number of primes occurring in every row in the table on the previous page? Which decades have the least number of primes? Which have the most number of primes?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.417,
            0.667,
            0.439
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Primes through the Ages"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.441,
            0.87,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "Prime numbers are the building blocks of all whole numbers. Starting from the time of the Greek civilisation (more than 2000 years ago) to this day, mathematicians are still struggling to uncover their secrets!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.509,
            0.87,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": "**Food for thought:** is there a largest prime number? Or does the list of prime numbers go on without an end? A mathematician named Euclid found the answer and so will you in a later class!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.577,
            0.87,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": "**Fun fact:** The largest prime number that anyone has ‘written down’ is so large that it would take around 6500 pages to write it! So they could only write it on a computer!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.667,
            0.818,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": "4. Which of the following numbers are prime: 23, 51, 37, 26?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.69,
            0.884,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "5. Write three pairs of prime numbers less than 20 whose sum is a multiple of 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.735,
            0.884,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "The numbers 13 and 31 are prime numbers. Both these numbers have same digits 1 and 3. Find such pairs of prime numbers up to 100."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.781,
            0.841,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "Find seven consecutive composite numbers between 1 and 100."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.804,
            0.885,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "8. **Twin primes** are pairs of primes having a difference of 2. For example, 3 and 5 are twin primes. So are 17 and 19. Find the other twin primes between 1 and 100."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
